  "query_preview": "/Through Thursday/...\nIssued at 1113 PM MDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nRecent obs show temperatures starting to drop over parts of the \neastern plains, and most of the cloud cover from earlier today has \ncleared out. A Freeze Warning remains in effect until 9am Friday for \nthe eastern plains & I-25 corridor, where lows tonight are expected \nto drop into the upper 20s-low 30s. Winds should stay light across \nthe area overnight, with some breezier conditions (gusts of about 15-\n20mph) in the northeasternmost plains.\n\nCooler temperatures persist Friday, with highs in the mid to upper \n50s across the plains & I-25 corridor. Downsloping winds should keep \nlows tomorrow night above freezing for most of the foothills, while \nthe northern and easternmost plains should again see lows in the \nupper 20s-low 30s. Despite the good model agreement in below \nfreezing temperatures across the plains Friday night, we have \ndecided to hold off for now on issuing any frost or freeze products \nuntil we know tonight's low temperatures. Should a killing freeze \nhit tonight (low temps <= 28F), we would not issue another product; \nhowever, if lows don't quite reach that 28 degrees or less \nthreshold, we may be looking at another frost/freeze product.\n \nTemperatures warm back up on Saturday as a ridge moves into the \narea. Highs are expected to be in the upper 70s on Saturday as the \nridge axis moves across the state, with southwest flow dominating on \nthe upstream side of the ridge by Sunday, keeping temps on the \nwarmer side through the first half of next week and pulling in some \nmid-level moisture. While conditions should stay dry over the plains \nand urban corridor, we can't rule out a few afternoon rain/snow \nshowers over the higher terrain Sunday through Wednesday. \n\nCurrently, both the GFS and ECMWF deterministic solutions bring in \nour next trough Thursday or Friday. This far out, though, the timing \nof this system is highly uncertain, as is the location of the cutoff \nlow/trough axis. We'll see how the models trend and see if there's \nany better agreement from run to run and across models as we get \ncloser to the end of next week.",
